The pre-fix cross-crate run exposed two test-harness lifecycle defects rather
than product-state defects. Chain-only sync fixtures kept the reliable
capacity-one reorg receiver alive without starting its tx-pool consumer, so a
second transition had no possible progress. They now retire that dormant
capability through one named fixture boundary, with a static drift check and a
closed-consumer regression; production reliable delivery is unchanged. The RPC
example suite retained its baseline transaction while testing direct local
submission, depending on the deleted verify-queue acknowledgement gap. It now
establishes absence and observes the final direct Apply result.

The first complete managed-integration attempt passed 13 specs and then
stopped at `RbfContainInvalidInput`. The product returned the documented UAK
outcome: the candidate was terminally rejected, every Accepted owner survived,
and no replacement-history owner was created. The old integration assertion
instead required `develop`'s uncharged rejected-candidate conflict LRU. That
assertion was classified obsolete after a compatibility/security review, not
changed as a product workaround. `ARCHITECTURE.md` now records the intentional
semantic narrowing of `get_raw_tx_pool.conflicted`: only successfully displaced
Accepted victims retained as charged `ReplacementHistory` appear there;
per-hash recent-reject remains the failed-candidate surface. The exact failing
spec passed, followed by the complete affected eight-spec RBF/relay group.

The next full-universe attempt exposed a product defect at
`RbfReplaceProposedSuccess`: committing the parent of an Accepted RBF winner
published the parent's output as available at the chain layer even though the
winner still owned its pool spend. That false positive advanced the retained
victim's dependency level, woke Recovery and terminalized the victim under the
winner's still-live input. The root was layer ambiguity, not replacement
history or maintenance ordering. Chain receipts now name their values as raw
chain-layer facts, and chain Plan composes positive cell facts with the
already-compiled post-Apply membership spender projection before dependency
publication. No state, lock, cache, retry or repair scan was added. A
red-before-green model test fixes the exact parent-commit/winner-spend case;
the original integration and the five-spec successful/rejected/concurrent/
recovery RBF group pass.

That semantic group also exposed an obsolete `RbfConcurrency` assertion. The
old test required every concurrent policy loser to enter `conflicted`, which
was the retired uncharged conflict-cache behavior and is scheduling-dependent:
only a contender that actually became Accepted and was then displaced owns a
committed `replaced by tx` outcome. The test now derives the exact charged
history set from those committed outcomes while retaining the highest-fee
winner and terminal-status assertions. Direct losers remain recent rejects.

